## Heat, Soaking, And Controlled Processing

### διπλοῦν ἀγγεῖον μυρεψικόν / δίπλωμα
<a id="term-diployn-aggeion-myrepsikon-diploma"></a>

Meaning: Double vessel; a smaller container set inside a larger hot-water vessel.

Why it matters: This is the clearest ancient perfume analogue to controlled indirect heating.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-diployn-aggeion-myrepsikon-diploma)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-diployn-aggeion-myrepsikon-diploma)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Ancient perfume makers used indirect heat.
- Galen explicitly explains the double-vessel setup.
- Perfumes were heated this way because harsher heating could damage their potency.

Do not infer:
- That every heating stage used a double vessel.

### κακκάβη
<a id="term-kakkabe"></a>

Meaning: Cauldron or cooking pot; sometimes the outer vessel in indirect heating.

Why it matters: Connects everyday boiling hardware with more controlled perfumery practice.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-kakkabe)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-kakkabe)
Citations: 3

Safe claims:
- Cauldrons were used both for direct boiling and as part of a water-bath arrangement.
- Some recipes distinguish tinned from untinned vessels for different effects.

### χαλκός
<a id="term-chalkos"></a>

Meaning: Copper or bronze vessel in perfumery contexts.

Why it matters: Strong post angle on metal choice and active vessel behavior.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-chalkos)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-chalkos)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Bronze or copper vessels appear in boiling stages.
- In some later texts, vessel material is not neutral but part of the intended process result.

